# No Hardcoded User-Facing Copy

English is the default product language. French and Spanish must stay in sync for every user-facing string.

## Rails (backend)

- Any new or edited user-facing string in ERB views, mailers, controllers (flash), or similar must go through `t()` / `I18n.t`.
- Add the key to `en`, `fr`, and `es` in the same change (see `i18n-locale-sync`).
- Do not leave French or English literals in product views when a translation key can be used.
- Internal-only debug tools may keep hardcoded copy when they are not end-user UI.
- Verify with `bundle exec rspec spec/lib/locale_completeness_spec.rb` before finishing.

## Android

- Any new or edited user-facing string in Kotlin (Toasts, dialogs, notifications, `setText`, widget RemoteViews) or layout XML (`android:text`, `android:hint`) must use a `@string/` / `R.string.*` resource.
- Add the key to all three buckets in the same change:
  - `android/app/src/main/res/values/strings.xml` (English default / fallback)
  - `android/app/src/main/res/values-fr/strings.xml`
  - `android/app/src/main/res/values-es/strings.xml`
- Keep the three files on the same `name=` key set.
- Prefer reusing an existing key over inventing a near-duplicate.

## Product copy tone

Follow `user-facing-ui-copy`: describe outcomes for end users; never put crypto, infra, or developer jargon in locale / string resources.
